course: biology
lesson: 55
title: Sleep and hibernation
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every animal needs rest, but they rest in amazing ways: dolphins sleep with half their brain, giraffes nap standing up, bats sleep upside down, and koalas sleep most of the day.
explain: Hibernation is something much bigger than a long nap: the body cools down, the heart slows from racing to crawling, and the animal lives on its stored fat through the hungry time of winter.
explain: Picture a hedgehog curled under a pile of leaves in November; its heart drops from about 190 beats a minute to around 20, and it stays almost switched off until spring, which is why bonfire piles must be checked before lighting.
explain: Some animals sleep through summer instead: the African lungfish digs into drying mud, wraps itself in slime and waits for the rain, and that is called aestivation.
explain: Cartoons show bears snoring all winter and waking up in spring with a big stretch, and goldfish forgetting everything in 3 seconds; real bears sleep more lightly than hedgehogs, and goldfish remember for months.
fact: Hedgehogs hibernate from about November to March, when there are few beetles, worms or slugs to eat; the heartbeat drops from about 190 to around 20 beats a minute, and they may wake and move nests a few times.
fact: Bears sleep through winter in dens living on their fat; their temperature drops only a little, mothers give birth in the den and make milk for the cubs from their stored fat, a bear wakes up thin and hungry in spring, and it can go months without eating, drinking or going to the toilet.
fact: The hazel dormouse can hibernate for six months or more, after fattening up on hazelnuts and berries in autumn.
fact: Dolphins sleep with half their brain at a time and one eye open, so they can still come up to breathe and watch for danger.
fact: Giraffes sleep very little, in short naps, mostly standing, because getting up takes a long time with lions around; koalas sleep about 18 to 22 hours a day, because their gum leaves give little energy.
fact: Bats sleep hanging upside down with their foot tendons locked, and in cold countries they hibernate in caves and attics; bats are not blind.
fact: Common frogs spend winter in mud or under logs, and some males rest at the bottom of ponds, taking in oxygen through their skin.
fact: The North American wood frog can freeze solid in winter, with its heart stopped, and thaw and hop away in spring.
fact: Dogs, cats and many other mammals and birds have REM sleep, the stage when people dream, so dogs may dream too, though we cannot ask them.
fact: Children of 9 to 10 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night; during sleep the body grows and repairs itself, and the brain sorts memories; bright light from a screen before bed tells the brain it is still daytime, so sleep comes harder.
fact: Fish have no eyelids, but many rest by slowing down and floating still; some parrotfish sleep in a bubble of slime.
fact: Mediterranean tortoises hibernate, and owners guided by a vet keep them steady at about 5 degrees so they neither wake early nor freeze.
fact: Waking up from hibernation burns a lot of stored fat that has to last the winter, and there is no food to replace it, so hibernating hedgehogs, bats and tortoises must be left alone.
fact: African lungfish survive the dry season sealed in slime in dried mud, breathing air, sometimes for years; this summer sleep is aestivation.
exercise: Why does a hedgehog hibernate in winter? | There are few beetles, worms or slugs to eat, so it saves energy | It is too cold to keep its spines up, so it has to lie down until spring | It is hiding from the Moon | It needs the time to grow new spines
exercise: What happens to a hibernating hedgehog's heartbeat? | It drops from about 190 to around 20 beats a minute | It stops completely all winter and starts again in spring | It speeds up to keep it warm | It stays exactly the same
exercise: Why does a brown bear come out of its den thin and hungry in spring? | It lived on its own fat all winter without eating | It ate nothing but snow and ice all winter long | Its cubs ate its food | It ran around inside the den
exercise: What does a hazel dormouse do in autumn before it hibernates? | Fattens up on hazelnuts and berries | Flies south to Africa | Builds a nest at the top of a pine tree | Stops eating to get light
exercise: How does a dolphin sleep without drowning? | Half its brain sleeps at a time, so it still comes up to breathe | It takes one huge breath and holds it for the whole night until dawn | It sleeps lying on the beach | It breathes through gills while asleep
exercise: Why does a giraffe usually rest standing up? | Getting up takes a long time, which is dangerous with lions around | Its legs are stiff and cannot bend at all, so it could never lie down | It is too tall to fit on the ground | Giraffes never sleep at all
exercise: Why can a bat hang upside down asleep without getting tired? | Its foot tendons lock, so hanging takes no effort | It holds on tightly with its teeth | It is glued by its own spit | It wakes up every minute or so to grip on again
exercise: How can a frog sleep at the bottom of a pond in winter? | It takes in oxygen through its skin | It holds one breath from autumn to spring | It breathes through a reed like a snorkel | It has gills like a fish all its life
exercise: Why does a pet dog twitch and whimper while asleep? | It is in a sleep stage when it may be dreaming | It is cold and shivering | It is only pretending to be asleep to get a treat | It is chasing fleas in its fur
exercise: About how many hours of sleep a night does a child of 9 or 10 need? | About 9 to 12 hours | About 4 to 5 hours | About 6 hours | About 15 to 18 hours
exercise: Why does a goldfish hang still near the bottom of the tank at night? | It is resting, the fish way of sleeping | It is ill and about to die | It has forgotten how to swim in the dark | It is hiding from the filter
exercise: Why does an expert keep a hibernating tortoise at about 5 degrees? | So it does not wake too early or freeze | So it grows a thicker, harder shell before spring | So it stays fresh to eat | Because tortoises like ice
exercise: What does an African lungfish do when its pond dries up? | It seals itself in slime in the mud and breathes air until rain comes | It walks to the sea on its fins | It dries out completely and comes back to life later as a new egg | It digs down to an underground river
exercise: What is aestivation? | Sleeping through a hot, dry season | Sleeping all through the daytime | Sleeping upside down | Waking up in spring
exercise: How long does a koala sleep each day? | About 18 to 22 hours | About 2 hours | About 8 hours | It never sleeps at all
exercise: What does the body do while a child sleeps? | Grows, repairs itself and sorts memories | Switches off the brain completely until morning | Stops the heart for a rest | Uses up all the food at once
test: cartoon bear snores all winter then wakes up fresh as anything. legit? | Not quite, bears sleep lightly and wake up thin and hungry | Yes, they wake up fatter than before | Yes, and they eat every day in the den from a store of fish they hid | No, bears never sleep in winter
test: my brother reckons goldfish forget everything in 3 seconds. true? | No, that is a myth, goldfish can remember for months | Yes, so they never get bored going round the bowl | Yes, but only at night | No, they forget everything in 3 days
test: Why should you never disturb a hibernating hedgehog? | Waking up burns its fat store, which it needs to last the winter | It will bite you with poison | It will stay awake for ever and never be able to sleep again | It will roll away and get lost
test: Why should a bonfire pile be checked before it is lit in November? | A hedgehog may be hibernating inside | Hedgehogs light bonfires | Bats hibernate only in bonfire piles | The leaves might be too wet
test: How can a mother bear feed her cubs when she has not eaten for months? | She makes milk from her stored fat | She catches fish in her sleep | The cubs bring her food | The cubs do not need food till spring
test: What would happen to a dolphin if it fell completely asleep underwater? | It could forget to come up and breathe | It would sink to the bottom and sleep happily | It would grow gills | Nothing, dolphins breathe water
test: Why might an animal that eats poor food sleep longer? | Its food gives little energy, so it saves energy by resting | Poor food makes animals sleepy because of sugar | It is too sad to stay awake | It is waiting for better food to fall from the sky into its nest
test: why the hell is the bat flapping around in winter? mum woke it moving boxes in the loft | Waking in winter wastes fat it needs, so roosts must be left alone | Bats like to be woken up for a bit of exercise now and then | It's fine, bats eat snow in winter | It's not a bat, bats are blind
test: A wood frog froze solid in winter and its heart stopped. What happens in spring? | It thaws and hops away | It stays frozen for ever | It turns into a tadpole again | It only comes back if someone warms it in their hands
test: Why does staring at a bright screen before bed make it harder to fall asleep? | Bright light tells the brain it is still daytime | Screens make your eyes too heavy | Screens use up all of the brain's memory | It doesn't, screens help you sleep better
test: How is sleeping through summer heat like hibernating through winter cold? | Both help an animal live through a time without food or water | Both happen only in the Arctic | Both make the animal grow faster | Neither has anything to do with food, they are only about the weather
test: Why is a warm house dangerous for a tortoise that ought to be hibernating? | It may wake and burn its energy with no food to eat | Warm air dries its shell into dust | Tortoises catch colds from radiators and hot water pipes | It is not dangerous at all
exam: Identify the principal physiological change observed in the hedgehog during hibernation. | A marked reduction in heart rate and body temperature | An increase in heart rate to generate heat against the cold | A cessation of all cardiac activity | The growth of a thicker coat of spines
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sleep of the dolphin? | One half of the brain rests while the other remains alert | Both halves of the brain rest simultaneously upon the seabed | The dolphin does not sleep at any point | The dolphin sleeps exclusively upon the shore
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the nightly sleep requirement of a child aged nine or ten years. | Approximately nine to twelve hours | Approximately four to five hours | Approximately fifteen to eighteen hours | No sleep is required at that age
exam: Ascertain the term applied to a period of dormancy undertaken during a hot and arid season. | Aestivation | Hibernation | Migration | Metamorphosis
